<sn><md>### Human Health Consequences of Mutagenic Pollutants

| Health consequence | What happens | Possible result |
|---|---|---|
| `Cancer risk` | Long-term exposure to certain mutagens in air or water can damage DNA in body cells. | Increased risk of cancers, including lung, skin, liver, and bladder cancer, depending on the pollutant and exposure. |
| `Heritable genetic defects` | Mutations in germ cells—sperm or eggs—can be passed to offspring. | Offspring may inherit genetic changes that can cause metabolic or developmental disorders. |
| `Neurological and hormonal disruption` | Some pollutants, including heavy metals and certain organic chemicals, can damage the nervous system or interfere with hormones. | Problems with brain function, development, or hormone regulation. |

**Note:** Neurological and hormonal effects are not necessarily caused by mutations; pollutants can also produce them through other mechanisms.</md></sn>